"B3.2"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=100%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Liver Vascular Abnormality:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma, prolonged whole-blood clotting time\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, dizziness, fainting, hypotension\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst with Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, dizziness, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n\nBiliary Tract Obstruction vs Peliosis Hepatis (Hepatic Vascular Malformation with Cystic Spaces):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nLiver Vascular Abnormality vs Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability:\n  Favours first:  bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, dizziness, fainting, hypotension\n\nLiver Vascular Abnormality vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst with Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nLiver Vascular Abnormality vs Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, dizziness, fainting, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n\nLiver Vascular Abnormality vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Hepatic Vein Thrombosis):\n  Favours first:  bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma, prolonged whole-blood clotting time\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome\n\nLiver Vascular Abnormality vs Peliosis Hepatis (Hepatic Vascular Malformation with Cystic Spaces):\n  Favours first:  bruising susceptibility, capillary fragility, gastrointestinal hemorrhage, joint hemorrhage, persistent bleeding after trauma\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability vs Ruptured Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst with Hemoperitoneum:\n  Favours first:  abdominal tenderness, fainting, hypotension, pallor, rebound tenderness\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever, increased urinary frequency\n\nRuptured Ectopic Pregnancy with Hemodynamic Instability vs Ovarian Torsion with Secondary Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abdominal tenderness, hypotension, pallor, rebound tenderness\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, difficulty emptying bowel, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever, frequent urge to urinate"}
